WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:55.093
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:55.093 --> 00:00:56.630
Sal Khan here from Khan Academy.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:01:01.287
Welcome to our special
Homeroom edition on college,

00:01:01.287 --> 00:01:03.000
"Is College Right For Me,"

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.340
or I guess is college right for you.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.050
We have a very exciting
group of panelists,

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:09.690
young people who are maybe a few years

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.790
ahead of many of y'all who
are expert in several domains,

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.550
but especially expert in
thinking about college

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:17.990
when it's right, what
other options there are

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:19.500
and how to make the most of it,

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.750
but before we jump into that,

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.430
I will give my standard reminders.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:24.700
First of all, we are a not-for-profit

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:26.040
and if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.460
please think about going
to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.000
It's because of your donations,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.030
if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:34.250
that make Khan Academy possible to reach

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:36.950
hundreds of millions of
folks around the world.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:38.550
I also want to give a special shout out

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.950
to several organizations that
have gone above and beyond

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:42.980
in supporting Khan Academy,

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:46.300
especially since the seemingly
never ending pandemic started

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.270
over a year and a half ago.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:49.880
A special thanks to Bank of America,

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.690
AT&amp;T, Google.org,

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:54.500
Novartis, Fastly, and General Motors,

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.130
and the many other
supporters across the board

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:00.190
who have contributed to
Khan Academy to allow

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:02.600
us to do the work that we do.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.430
So, and final announcement,

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:07.530
there is a podcast version
of this live stream

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:08.980
wherever you get your podcasts:

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:11.420
Homeroom with Sal, The Podcast.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.630
So I encourage you to
check that out, as well.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:16.130
So with that out of the way,

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:20.130
I am super excited to
introduce our panelists

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:21.900
and I will actually ask them
to introduce themselves,

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.670
but we have Delisha, who
is a cybersecurity expert

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:26.540
and also recent college grad.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.248
We have Madison, who is a designer artist

00:02:30.248 --> 00:02:34.010
associated with CNN+ and
also a recent college grad.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.360
And we have Ernest, who is a
software engineer at Google

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.907
and founder and president of Code House,

00:02:39.907 --> 00:02:41.170
and also a recent college grad.

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.920
But Delisha, let me start with you.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.220
Tell us a little bit about
who you are and your journey

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.263
into becoming a cybersecurity expert.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:51.303
- Yeah, Sal.

00:02:51.303 --> 00:02:52.770
Thank you, I'm so glad to be here.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:53.930
Hi everyone out there.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:55.250
I am Delisha Hodo,

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.460
an undergraduate
cybersecurity student advisor,

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:58.830
as Sal stated.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:02.080
In my career, I have the
pleasure of advising,

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:06.512
as well as supporting
students and future defenders

00:03:06.512 --> 00:03:09.407
in the cybersecurity space.

00:03:09.407 --> 00:03:12.830
And in that work, we're making
sure that they are trained

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:15.680
so that they can not only defend our world

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:16.720
in the cybersecurity world,

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.993
but also defend you and
the different things

00:03:18.993 --> 00:03:20.700
that you love to do.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.580
When I'm not advising, I am the
chair of our Sans HBCU team,

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.560
where our goal is to
support juniors and seniors

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.520
at historically black
colleges and universities

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:33.980
receive free cybersecurity training

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.180
and certifications, as well.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.440
And honestly, what led me to
this path of cybersecurity

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.870
was honestly by chance.

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:44.210
I actually majored in psychology,

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.170
got my bachelor's in psychology.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:49.100
And it's one of those
majors where it allows you

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:51.430
to go anywhere and end up anywhere.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.960
And I'm one of those
people that pretty much,

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.180
I would say cybersecurity found me

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:59.140
and I wouldn't have it any other way,

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:02.350
as it allows me to watch other students

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:04.108
and other individuals achieve greatness

00:04:04.108 --> 00:04:06.970
while also defending the world

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.450
from cyber attacks and bad actors,

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:12.650
as well as supporting others and hopefully

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.820
inspiring more of you out
there to help us defend

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:18.313
and become cybersecurity experts, as well.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.179
- I love that, Delisha.

00:04:21.179 --> 00:04:22.760
And I'm gonna ask you many
more questions about it,

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:25.370
especially because, at
least what you're advising

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:26.450
these undergraduate students to do,

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:28.400
it's like they could get to
live in a Tom Clancy novel.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.470
They get to like defend the country

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:33.890
in the cyber world, which
is obviously only becoming

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:36.080
a bigger and bigger thing,

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:36.913
but let's go to you Ernest.

00:04:36.913 --> 00:04:38.840
Tell me a little bit about your journey

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.640
and how you ended up both
as a software engineer,

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.423
but also what you're doing at Code House.

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:46.783
- Sure.

00:04:46.783 --> 00:04:48.930
So thanks for the
introduction, glad to be here.

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:50.550
Hello, everyone.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:51.900
My name is Ernest Holmes.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:55.540
I grew up in New Jersey and
I made it all the way down

00:04:55.540 --> 00:05:00.124
to Atlanta to attend college
at Morehouse College.

00:05:00.124 --> 00:05:02.900
Morehouse College is an
historically Black institution

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.130
serving all Black men.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:07.370
And while at Morehouse,

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:10.850
I was very aware about
the many opportunities

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:12.890
that exist in the tech industry.

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:14.200
And when I was just a freshman,

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.520
I actually wound up getting
an internship at Google

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:18.360
to be a software engineer.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:22.130
So I spent every summer actually
while I was at Morehouse

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:24.360
interning at Google out in California.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:25.730
So they flew me out,

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:29.080
got to work on amazing
projects from advertisements,

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:31.278
mobile to development,

00:05:31.278 --> 00:05:34.170
to artificial intelligence
and machine learning

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:35.773
on these robotic flowers.

00:05:36.867 --> 00:05:38.730
And that eventually allowed me to become

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:41.477
a full-time software engineer at Google

00:05:41.477 --> 00:05:42.310
working on the fuchsia team.

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:43.820
So that's my day job.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:45.650
That's what I do, and I'm very passionate

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:47.410
about being in the tech industry,

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:50.740
but I also realized that there was a need

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:54.070
for more people of color to be exposed to,

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:55.810
especially at an earlier age,

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:58.770
to the different opportunities that exist

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.730
in the tech industry,
whether it's engineering

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:03.370
or design, product management.

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:06.300
And that allowed me to get together,

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:07.350
not only with my sister,

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:08.970
but one of my best
friends, Travis Thompson,

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:10.520
and the other person on this call,

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.070
Madison Metivier, and a bunch of other

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:15.073
great individuals to make Code House,

00:06:15.073 --> 00:06:15.970
which is all about
exposing students of color

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:17.023
to the tech industry.

00:06:18.017 --> 00:06:19.160
So we expose middle and
high school students

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:21.100
to different careers in the tech industry,

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:24.700
but then now we also support
early college students

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:27.010
with scholarships, that's
right, scholarships.

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:29.130
So we can actually pay for your schooling

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:30.800
if you're interested in
going into tech industry

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.170
and attending an HBCU.

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:34.697
So that's a little bit about me,

00:06:34.697 --> 00:06:36.470
and I guess the last hat I wear right now,

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:39.620
I'm also actually a professor
at Morehouse College,

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:41.180
teaching freshmen computer science.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:43.513
So wearing a few different hats right now.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:46.940
- I want to ask you more
questions about that.

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:48.410
We actually already
have questions coming in

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:49.243
through YouTube.

00:06:49.243 --> 00:06:51.500
I encourage anyone watching to
start putting your questions

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:53.600
for any of these very
impressive young people

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:55.470
that you see in front of you.

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:57.700
But I have one question
before I move on to Madison,

00:06:57.700 --> 00:06:59.620
what does the fuchsia team work on?

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.790
Fuchsia is just a color.

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:03.608
What is the fuchsia team?

00:07:03.608 --> 00:07:05.000
(Ernest laughing)

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:06.690
- I'm glad you asked.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:09.870
Yeah, fuchsia is actually
an operating system

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:13.270
that we're building from
scratch completely open source,

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:16.450
and it's actually one of
the first large developments

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:18.950
of operating systems
that's non Linux-based.

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:22.110
So a little background, every single thing

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:24.470
that works right now, computers, phones,

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:27.420
they're all derived and based
in this thing called Linux.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.450
We decided to make a operating system

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:31.050
that is not based on Linux.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.070
So it's rethinking how computers work

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:36.270
and how eventually different devices

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:38.000
can interact with one another.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.930
Super cool project, super cool team,

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:43.243
and hopefully you see a
lot more fuchsia soon.

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:47.200
- And I'm still wondering how
it's connected to the color,

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:50.090
but maybe we'll get into
that at another point.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:55.155
But Madison, tell us a
little bit about your journey

00:07:55.155 --> 00:07:59.870
on how you got to where you are
as a designer and an artist.

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:01.280
- Yeah well, hey everyone.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:04.140
Good afternoon, good evening,
good morning wherever you are.

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:05.770
My name is Madison Metivier.

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:10.100
I am a proud and loud 2019
graduate of Howard University,

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:13.660
earning a bachelor's
in fine arts in design

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:16.560
and a double minor in
art history and German.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:18.200
So that love for art actually brought me

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:19.210
to my current position.

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:24.090
I am a graphic artist, slash
production designer at CNN+.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:26.040
For those who may not
be familiar with CNN+,

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:27.900
it's totally okay.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:30.145
It actually launches next year.

00:08:30.145 --> 00:08:32.010
It's CNN's streaming platform.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:35.890
So kind of like an HBO
Max, a Netflix, but CNN.

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:38.990
So what that compiles
is a couple of things

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:41.490
I can't talk about, but very generally

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:44.830
it's a bunch of unique TV programming

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:47.030
that you can access on
a streaming platform.

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:49.530
Well, that's my day job.

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:52.790
After 6:00 PM, I kind of switch hats,

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:54.930
and I'm not only the creative director

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:56.110
at Code House Corporation,

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:58.670
as Ernest so eloquently explained,

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:01.870
but I'm also owner of my own
creative agency, Ojii Creative,

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:05.273
where we work with
brands such as Ruby Rose.

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:11.730
It's been a crazy wild ride
with the amounts of clients

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.310
and types from small
businesses to larger ones,

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:17.430
such as Quality Control
Music, Universal Music Group,

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:20.173
as well as community
centers in the Dallas area.

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:24.100
And yeah, my love of art
really brought me here

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:25.723
to be quite honest with you.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:28.093
- That's incredible.

00:09:28.093 --> 00:09:30.475
You know, I'd like to think that I was

00:09:30.475 --> 00:09:33.240
doing pretty well for
myself in my early twenties,

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:35.560
but y'all are making me
feel somewhat insecure

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:36.870
about what I was doing with myself,

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:39.700
given all that y'all have
already accomplished,

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:44.700
but the topic of today is
College, Is It Right For Me,

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:47.780
or is it right for the folks listening?

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:49.870
And you can imagine there's
a lot of young people,

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:52.070
some of whom are are saying,
"I want to go to college".

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:54.377
Some folks are saying, "I'm not sure.

00:09:54.377 --> 00:09:55.970
"There might be other paths."

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:57.210
And then even amongst the people

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.257
who are thinking about
college, they're saying,

00:09:59.257 --> 00:10:00.467
"Can I afford it?

00:10:00.467 --> 00:10:02.207
"How do I figure out how to navigate it?

00:10:02.207 --> 00:10:04.480
"What types of colleges
should I go through?"

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:07.470
So I'd like to go one by one,
three all again, and say,

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:08.630
how were you thinking about it

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:10.170
when you were 16 or 17 years old?

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:11.710
Did you have some of these doubts?

00:10:11.710 --> 00:10:13.070
How did you navigate these doubts?

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:14.120
Was it random chance?

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:15.330
Were there certain things you did

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:17.550
that helped you make better decisions?

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.440
And actually I'll go to the same order.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:20.890
Delisha, I'll start with you.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.470
- Yeah, for me, I was a first gen,

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:26.270
so first in my family to actually

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:28.350
attend college and graduate.

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:32.940
So for me, it was a lot of
exploration, a lot of thinking.

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:37.030
I initially actually wanted
to go into audio engineering,

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:40.540
so leaning into music
production because I love music

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:42.260
and I'm a very creative person.

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:44.930
And I soon discovered, and it's something

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:47.373
that you're gonna speak about
and you spoke a little bit to,

00:10:47.373 --> 00:10:49.260
that financial piece.

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:52.437
I sadly discovered that financially

00:10:52.437 --> 00:10:54.120
the school wasn't a fit for me

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:57.230
and I wasn't gonna be able
to go into audio engineering.

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:01.040
So that's when I made that
pivot to my plan B, psychology,

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:03.320
and haven't looked back since.

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:08.320
But for me I'll say, it's a unique path,

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:12.560
especially if you are a future
first-generation student

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.340
out there in the world and
you're the first in your family,

00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:16.830
there's a lot of resources out there.

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:18.850
There's a lot of people
that you can lean on.

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:22.890
And what I will say to you is
life is not a straight path.

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:26.460
So don't be worried if you do not end up

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:27.910
in a career in your major.

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:31.367
That's pretty normal right now
in the world that we live in.

00:11:31.367 --> 00:11:32.600
But that doesn't mean
that you're a failure.

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:36.040
What it means is you were
brave enough to take a chance

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:38.490
and that you still have
light years ahead of you

00:11:38.490 --> 00:11:40.150
to discover what your passion is

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:43.080
and to do something that makes you happy.

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:43.913
- And to make sure I understand,

00:11:43.913 --> 00:11:45.110
you were originally thinking
about audio engineering

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:46.180
and you decided to move out of that

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:48.080
because it was at a different school

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:49.100
and that was too expensive,

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:50.770
or you were thinking that the career

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:52.280
might be harder to attain?

00:11:52.280 --> 00:11:54.113
How did make that transition.

00:11:58.180 --> 00:12:00.620
- Initially, 'cause I'm
in the state of Georgia,

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:02.750
there was only one college
that actually offered

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:05.330
anything near to audio engineering,

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:08.620
and that college was Savannah
College of Art and Design.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:11.440
It's a private college, so with that,

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:14.590
it was extremely expensive,
and again, as I said before,

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:16.550
I'm first gen, first in my family.

00:12:16.550 --> 00:12:18.980
We're not the richest in the world.

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:21.530
So when I received that
financial aid package,

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:23.677
I realized, "Wow, even with a scholarship,

00:12:23.677 --> 00:12:28.677
"I can't afford $30,000 a
year or however much it was."

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:31.227
So I had to make that
conscious decision to say,

00:12:31.227 --> 00:12:35.057
"You know what, I do like
psychology, so let me pivot.

00:12:35.057 --> 00:12:37.807
"My plan B is gonna be I'm
gonna pivot to psychology.

00:12:37.807 --> 00:12:39.617
"I'm gonna see where it takes me,

00:12:39.617 --> 00:12:41.837
"and if it's meant to be, I'll always find

00:12:41.837 --> 00:12:44.689
"my way back to music,
whether it's through

00:12:44.689 --> 00:12:47.137
"audio engineering or just
finding ways to express myself

00:12:47.137 --> 00:12:49.957
"in other ways, whether through
spoken word, which I do,

00:12:49.957 --> 00:12:53.947
"poetry, or just enjoying
listening to music

00:12:53.947 --> 00:12:55.280
"and analyzing it."

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:57.000
So you can still find those joys

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:00.060
without necessarily going
into that major or career.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:02.570
So that's kind of how I was able to pivot

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:04.230
and change my mindset about it.

00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:06.370
And quite frankly, didn't
really have a choice

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:08.210
because of the financial situation,

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:10.210
but I feel like at the end of
the day, looking back on it,

00:13:10.210 --> 00:13:13.740
it was the right choice to
make and not go into debt,

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:16.220
as much debt doing that.

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:19.230
And as always, because I'm still young,

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:21.530
if I really want it to go
back into audio engineering,

00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:22.663
I'm sure I could.

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:25.210
- No, and thanks for sharing that story

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:28.060
because I think students don't
hear those stories enough,

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:30.090
because so many times in a forum like this

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:32.557
people are like, "Oh,
just do whatever you want

00:13:32.557 --> 00:13:34.793
"and all of that" and
there are students saying,

00:13:34.793 --> 00:13:37.077
"But how am I gonna pay for that?"

00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:40.800
I remember being in a similar situation.

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:44.500
My family, we grew up
not a lot of resources.

00:13:44.500 --> 00:13:46.750
And I remember my sister
applying to a school

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:48.180
but I'm like, "How are we gonna pay for,"

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:50.170
and I think at the time,
this was in the '90s,

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:51.843
it was $25,000 a year.

00:13:51.843 --> 00:13:53.207
I was like, "That's
more than my mom makes.

00:13:53.207 --> 00:13:54.957
"That's twice what my mom makes in a year.

00:13:54.957 --> 00:13:56.250
"How are we going to do that?"

00:13:56.250 --> 00:13:57.083
And then my sister explained to me

00:13:57.083 --> 00:13:59.330
about the whole financial
aid system and all of that.

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:03.621
So to your story, it sounds like,

00:14:03.621 --> 00:14:05.750
it sounds like you did the right things.

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:07.010
You said, "What am I interested in?"

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:08.360
But you'll explore the options.

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:10.770
You said, "Okay, now
what are they offering?"

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:12.520
They might be offering a
little bit of scholarship,

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:13.353
a little bit of financial aid,

00:14:13.353 --> 00:14:14.700
but it still was not practical,

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:16.670
but there's other really good options.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:18.650
And obviously now you
are helping other people

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:20.984
defend our country against cyber threats

00:14:20.984 --> 00:14:22.923
and whatever else.

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:27.410
Ernest, how did you think about
this when you were 16 or 17?

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:28.580
Did you always know you were going to be

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:29.500
a software engineer?

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:31.490
Did you always know you
were going to go to college?

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:33.050
Did you always think it
was going to be Morehouse?

00:14:33.050 --> 00:14:34.300
How do you navigate that?

